# Paqto

Async-first primitives for transport-agnostic peer communication.

## LAN example

```python
from paqto import Message, PaqtoConfig, PaqtoNode, Serializer
from paqto.lan import LanDiscovery, LanTransport


class JsonSerializer(Serializer):
    ...


node_a = PaqtoNode(
    name="node-a",
    transport=LanTransport(),
    discovery=LanDiscovery(),
    serializer=JsonSerializer(),
    config=PaqtoConfig(discover_timeout=2),
)
node_b = PaqtoNode(
    name="node-b",
    transport=LanTransport(),
    discovery=LanDiscovery(),
    serializer=JsonSerializer(),
)


@node_b.on_message("greeting")
def on_message(message: Message) -> None:
    print(message.payload)


await node_a.start()
await node_b.start()
peers = await node_a.discover(timeout=2)
target = next(peer for peer in peers if peer.peer.id == node_b.peer.id)
await node_a.send(target, {"text": "hello over LAN"}, type="greeting")
await node_a.stop()
await node_b.stop()
```

See `examples/lan_two_nodes.py` for a complete runnable version.

## Connection security metadata

Every `Connection` exposes transport-neutral `security_info` alongside
`local_endpoint`, `remote_endpoint`, and `is_closed`. The default
`SecurityInfo` reports no encryption or authentication guarantees; transports
may override it after establishing any security mechanism they support. Paqto
does not require TLS or any other specific mechanism.

## TLS on the LAN transport

TLS is enabled explicitly by passing `TlsConfig` to `LanTransport`. A plain
`LanTransport()` remains available for compatibility and controlled test
networks.

```python
from paqto import PaqtoConfig
from paqto.lan import LanTransport, TlsConfig

tls = TlsConfig(
    certfile="/path/to/node-certificate.pem",
    keyfile="/path/to/node-private-key.pem",
    cafile="/path/to/trusted-ca.pem",
    require_client_certificate=True,  # require mTLS for incoming connections
    handshake_timeout=10,             # bound the TLS handshake in both directions
)
transport = LanTransport(tls=tls)
```

Once TLS is requested, outgoing certificate-chain verification and hostname
verification are enabled by default. If `cafile` is omitted, Python's system
trust store is used. Disabling verification requires both
`verify_peer=False` and `check_hostname=False`; Paqto never applies that
downgrade implicitly. TLS uses Python's `ssl` module and accepts TLS 1.2 or
newer. The TLS handshake has a finite 10-second default deadline; it is
configurable independently from the subsequent Paqto protocol handshake.

Certificate verification does not by itself define a Paqto `Peer.id`. An
optional `peer_identity_resolver` maps the already verified certificate
mapping to a logical identity without imposing an X.509 naming convention in
Paqto. Applications that require every READY session to have a matching
authenticated identity can opt in:

```python
config = PaqtoConfig(require_authenticated_peer_id_match=True)
```

Discovery announcements remain unauthenticated hints. A discovered peer id is
never copied into `SecurityInfo.authenticated_peer_id`; that value can only
come from the established security mechanism.

## Paqto protocol handshake

`PaqtoNode` does not treat a transport connection as an application session.
Every incoming and outgoing connection now follows this lifecycle:

```text
transport connection -> optional transport security -> Paqto handshake
                     -> READY session -> application messages
```

Protocol version 1 uses the identifier `PAQTO`. Both peers exchange one small
JSON `hello` control frame containing the protocol version, logical `peer_id`,
capabilities, serializer encoding id, maximum serialized message size, and
optional protocol metadata. JSON is used only for the fixed control hello;
application messages continue to use the configured `Serializer`.

Paqto frames have a one-byte kind marker inside the existing transport frame:
control frames are consumed by the protocol layer, while application frames
are passed to the serializer only after the session is ready. Incompatible
versions or encodings, malformed hello data, timeouts, and early application
frames fail the handshake and close the connection.

The default serializer id is derived from the serializer class. Deployments
that need a language-neutral or otherwise stable wire identifier should set it
explicitly. Protocol options are configured without coupling them to a
transport:

```python
config = PaqtoConfig(
    handshake_timeout=5,
    protocol_version=1,
    capabilities=("feature-a", "feature-b"),
    serializer_id="example/message-json-v1",
    max_message_size=1024 * 1024,
    protocol_metadata={"implementation": "example"},
)
```

`PaqtoNode.connect()` returns only after the handshake reaches READY. The
negotiated immutable `ProtocolSession` can be inspected with
`node.session_for(connection)`. Its `peer_id_authenticated` flag is true only
when the handshake id matches an identity authenticated by the connection's
security layer.

Discovery remains only a reachability hint. An outgoing hello must agree with
the intended discovered peer id, and any available
`SecurityInfo.authenticated_peer_id` must agree with the hello on both incoming
and outgoing connections. Without transport authentication, the hello
`peer_id` identifies the protocol session but is not cryptographic proof of
who controls the connection. `require_authenticated_peer_id_match=True` makes
such proof mandatory for every session accepted by that node.

For a security-sensitive LAN deployment, use certificate verification, mTLS,
an application-defined `peer_identity_resolver`, and
`require_authenticated_peer_id_match=True` together. TLS without the resolver
authenticates a certificate/hostname but does not bind it to a logical
`Peer.id`. Plain TCP and UDP discovery are not safe identity mechanisms against
an active network attacker.

## Request/reply correlation

`PaqtoNode.request()` sends a normal application `Message` and waits for a
reply whose `reply_to` equals the request `id`. The pending correlation is
registered before bytes are sent, so an immediate reply cannot race ahead of
the waiting future. `PaqtoNode.reply()` creates the correlated response and,
when called by a message handler, sends it on the same READY connection:

```python
@node_b.on_message("query")
async def handle_query(message: Message) -> None:
    await node_b.reply(message, {"value": 42}, type="query-result")


response = await node_a.request(
    target,
    {"key": "example"},
    type="query",
    timeout=5,
)
```

Replies are protocol-correlated application messages. A reply with an unknown,
expired, or connection-mismatched `reply_to` is consumed and ignored; it is not
dispatched as a new application request. Pending requests are removed after
success, timeout, cancellation, connection loss, and node shutdown. A timeout
raises `RequestTimeoutError`; connection loss or shutdown raises
`RequestError`. The configured default is `PaqtoConfig.request_timeout`.

Handlers run in a fixed pool of node-supervised workers, so the connection
reader remains separate from application code without creating one unbounded
task per message. Messages may be processed concurrently up to
`handler_concurrency`; each invocation still uses the normal ordered
`MessageRouter` handler list. Async handlers must cooperate with the event loop;
sync handlers should return quickly and must not perform blocking I/O on the
event-loop thread.

Request/reply provides in-memory correlation only. It does not provide durable
queues, retries, idempotency, duplicate suppression, transactions, or
exactly-once delivery. Without an authenticated transport/session identity, a
handshake `peer_id` still is not cryptographic proof of the replying party.

## Technical acknowledgements

Paqto can negotiate the `paqto.ack.v1` capability. Support is enabled by
default and can be disabled with
`PaqtoConfig(enable_acknowledgements=False)`. Waiting for an ACK is explicit:

```python
await node_a.send(
    target,
    payload,
    type="event",
    require_ack=True,
    acknowledgement_timeout=2,
)
```

An ACK is a Paqto control frame, consumed by the protocol layer and never sent
to application handlers. It confirms only that the remote Paqto reader
received, deserialized, and validated the message envelope. It does **not**
mean that an application handler completed successfully, that data was made
durable, or that any application operation succeeded. Paqto does not
deduplicate acknowledged messages and makes no exactly-once guarantee.
The ACK may be emitted before the bounded dispatch queue admits the message;
later queue rejection, shutdown, cancellation, or handler failure does not
retract it.

Request and reply also accept `require_ack=True`. If the session did not
negotiate ACK support, `AcknowledgementUnavailableError` is raised before
sending. A missing ACK raises `AcknowledgementTimeoutError`; connection loss or
shutdown raises `AcknowledgementError`. Pending ACK futures follow the same
unconditional cleanup rules as pending requests.

## Connection lifecycle and duplicate sessions

`ConnectionState` describes the logical connection to a peer as
`DISCONNECTED`, `CONNECTING`, `CONNECTED`, `RECONNECTING`, `CLOSING`, or
`CLOSED`. Inspect it with `node.connection_state(peer)`; obtain the one
canonical READY channel, when present, with `node.connection_for_peer(peer)`.
The state belongs to orchestration rather than a physical socket because a
reconnect creates a new `Connection` and a new protocol session.

Concurrent `connect()` calls for the same peer use one shared connection
attempt. Different peers are not serialized behind that attempt. If both peers
open toward each other at the same time, Paqto selects one physical channel by
a stable rule: the peer with the lexicographically smaller `Peer.id` keeps its
outbound channel, and the other peer keeps the corresponding inbound channel.
Duplicates in the same direction keep the first session that reached READY.
The losing channel is closed and never becomes an additional application
session.

`await node.disconnect(peer)` closes a session intentionally and suppresses
automatic reconnect until a later explicit `connect()`.

## Automatic reconnect

Reconnect is disabled by default. Enable it with an immutable
`ReconnectPolicy`:

```python
from paqto import PaqtoConfig, ReconnectPolicy

config = PaqtoConfig(
    reconnect=ReconnectPolicy(
        enabled=True,
        initial_delay=0.5,
        multiplier=2,
        maximum_delay=30,
        jitter=0.1,
        max_attempts=None,  # keep trying while the node runs
    )
)
```

Every attempt uses bounded exponential backoff and the latest fresh discovery
endpoint known for that peer. `max_attempts` may be a positive integer or
`None`. `node.stop()` cancels an in-progress connection attempt or backoff
immediately. A successfully reconnected transport must repeat its security
handshake, Paqto hello, identity checks, and capability negotiation; no
authentication or READY state is inherited from the old socket.

Reconnect restores only the session. Paqto does not automatically resend an
application message, ACK wait, or request. Pending operations belonging to the
lost connection fail before reconnect begins, and a reply on a later session
cannot complete an old request.

## Heartbeat and liveness

Set `heartbeat_interval` to enable Paqto PING/PONG liveness checks. PING and PONG
are negotiated `paqto.heartbeat.v1` control frames and never enter the
application serializer or handlers:

```python
config = PaqtoConfig(
    heartbeat_interval=15,
    heartbeat_timeout=5,
)
```

Recent inbound traffic proves liveness and suppresses an unnecessary PING. If
no traffic arrives for one interval, the node sends a PING and requires the
matching PONG within `heartbeat_timeout`. Failure closes the exact connection,
fails its pending operations, and may start the configured reconnect policy.

## Discovery freshness

`DiscoveredPeer.last_seen` is evaluated through `freshness()` / `is_fresh()`.
`PaqtoConfig.peer_ttl` defaults to 60 seconds and prevents an expired discovery
observation from opening a new connection or driving reconnect. `LanDiscovery`
also prunes its cache with a configurable `peer_ttl` (60 seconds by default)
and admits at most `max_discovered_peers` entries (1024 by default). Existing
entries can still be refreshed while the cache is full. Set either TTL to
`None` only when expiration is deliberately managed outside that layer.

TTL is a reachability rule, not an identity or authorization signal. A live
READY connection remains usable even if its last UDP observation expires;
discovery continues to be an unauthenticated hint.

## Backpressure, handler isolation, and limits

READY application messages cross a bounded node-wide inbound queue before
dispatch. Each READY connection also has one bounded outbound frame queue and
one writer, preserving frame order and bounding stored queue entries. The
default `BackpressurePolicy.WAIT` propagates pressure to the caller or network
reader; it does **not** bound how many tasks an application may itself create
while waiting. `REJECT` instead fails an outbound operation or closes an
inbound peer that exceeds the configured capacity. Applications must also
bound their own task creation. Neither policy adds persistence or delivery
retry.

An exception from an application handler becomes a `MessageRoutingError`, is
logged without the message payload, and emits `NodeEventType.HANDLER_ERROR`.
The default `HandlerErrorPolicy.CONTINUE` keeps the network session alive.
`CLOSE_CONNECTION` is available when a deployment deliberately wants a
handler failure to terminate only the associated peer connection. Handler
errors are never silently discarded.

The principal core limits are configured together:

```python
from paqto import BackpressurePolicy, HandlerErrorPolicy, PaqtoConfig

config = PaqtoConfig(
    max_message_size=1024 * 1024,       # negotiated application payload
    max_pending_requests=1024,
    max_pending_acknowledgements=1024,
    max_inbound_queue=256,
    max_outbound_queue=256,             # per READY connection
    max_connections=128,                # includes inbound handshakes
    handler_concurrency=4,
    handshake_timeout=5,
    idle_timeout=None,                  # disabled unless explicitly set
    inbound_backpressure=BackpressurePolicy.WAIT,
    outbound_backpressure=BackpressurePolicy.WAIT,
    handler_error_policy=HandlerErrorPolicy.CONTINUE,
)
```

`max_event_queue` bounds asynchronous observations as well. Events are
diagnostic and never apply backpressure to network processing: when that queue
is full an event is dropped with a warning. The concrete LAN transport's
`max_frame_size` independently bounds the complete transport frame and should
be at least large enough for the Paqto marker plus the configured application
limit. Its default is therefore the default Paqto message limit plus one byte.
`max_pending_accepts` (128 by default) bounds connections waiting inside the
TCP accept queue, and `frame_payload_timeout` (30 seconds by default) closes a
peer that declares a frame length but does not complete that payload.
`max_message_size` remains the transport-neutral, handshake-negotiated payload
limit.

These are count and per-item limits, not an aggregate memory budget. Inbound
and outbound queues have no byte quota, and a custom serializer may expand a
small wire payload into a much larger Python object. Production deployments
must choose a safe serializer and size `max_message_size`, queue counts, and
connection counts together. `frame_payload_timeout=None` deliberately removes
the partial-payload deadline and is not recommended for hostile peers.

`idle_timeout` closes a READY connection that receives no frame within the
configured period. Heartbeat PING/PONG counts as traffic; deployments using
both settings should leave enough idle budget for the heartbeat interval and
response timeout.

## Lifecycle events and logging

Applications can observe generic node events with sync or async listeners:

```python
from paqto import NodeEvent, NodeEventType


@node.on_event(NodeEventType.CONNECTED)
async def connected(event: NodeEvent) -> None:
    print(event.peer_id, event.connection_id)


@node.on_event()  # all event types
def observe(event: NodeEvent) -> None:
    ...
```

Events cover discovery/expiry observations, connection and reconnect
transitions, established authentication, disconnects, protocol failures,
transport/listener failures, handler failures, and resource-limit rejection.
Discovery expiry is evaluated when discovery/connect/reconnect activity
rechecks freshness; it is not a background authorization mechanism. A failing
event listener is logged and does not stop other listeners or network tasks.
Event objects contain generic identity, connection, error, and protocol
metadata, never an application payload.

Paqto uses Python's standard `logging` package under the `paqto` logger
hierarchy. Records add fields such as `paqto_local_peer_id`, `paqto_peer_id`,
`paqto_connection_id`, `paqto_message_id`, `paqto_message_type`, and
`paqto_error_type` when relevant. Payloads and headers are not logged by
default. Peer ids, message ids, and message types are still metadata and may be
sensitive in some deployments; do not put secrets in those identifiers and
configure log access accordingly.

`start()` rejects a second concurrent start, while `stop()` is idempotent. A
node may be started again after a completed stop. Shutdown cancels and gathers
accept, reader, dispatch, event, writer, heartbeat, and reconnect tasks; fails
pending request/ACK/frame futures; closes sessions, listener, discovery, and
transport resources; and discards bounded queue contents.

## Delivery guarantees and non-guarantees

Paqto provides framed transport sessions, exact protocol-version and serializer
matching, sender/recipient consistency with the READY session, optional
authenticated `Peer.id` binding, volatile routing, request/reply correlation,
optional technical receipt ACKs, liveness checks, and reconnect of the
connection itself. Within one READY TCP connection, the single writer preserves
the order in which frames leave Paqto's outbound queue.

The following stages are distinct:

1. A successful transport write means bytes were handed to the local transport;
   it does not prove remote receipt.
2. A Paqto ACK proves remote protocol receipt, deserialization, and envelope
   validation only.
3. Application success requires an application-defined response or other
   domain-specific confirmation.

Paqto does **not** provide exactly-once or at-least-once delivery, automatic
message resend, durable messaging, persistence, transactions, duplicate
suppression, global ordering, application-level success, authorization, or
reliable/auditable event delivery. Send timeout or cancellation also does not
prove that a queued frame was never transmitted. Reconnect establishes a new
session and never revives pending requests or ACK waits from the old one.

## Security and production status

The package remains pre-alpha. Plain `LanTransport()` is appropriate only for
controlled networks and tests. UDP discovery announcements are unauthenticated
and spoofable; an attacker can advertise a victim peer id or endpoint. Outgoing
handshake matching detects a discovery/hello mismatch, but only verified TLS
plus an identity resolver can establish who owns a `Peer.id`.

The built-in limits reduce application-level exhaustion but do not replace
firewalls, OS backlog/rate limits, certificate lifecycle controls, monitoring,
or authorization. In particular, TLS work happens before the node's
`max_connections` admission check, so a hostile connection flood must also be
controlled outside Paqto. Certificate revocation policy, pinning, private-key
rotation, and application authorization are deployment responsibilities.
Never use an unsafe deserializer (for example, untrusted pickle data): Paqto
does not sandbox serializer code.

The current code is suitable for controlled LAN evaluation and integration
testing. Treat a security-sensitive production release as conditional on a
strict TLS/mTLS identity profile, deployment-level flood controls, conservative
resource sizing, supported-Python CI, and an explicit review of the chosen
serializer and authorization model.

## Migration note

The retired threaded `paqto.connection.Connection(host, port)` implementation
is not emulated. `paqto.connection` remains an import path for the current
async abstract `Connection`, `ConnectionState`, and `SecurityInfo`, but old
constructor and blocking send/receive behavior are intentionally breaking as
part of the async transport architecture.
